  "title": "All Nodes Distance K in Binary Tree",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Tree bfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree bfs",
    "queue",
    "graph bfs"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "the tricky part isn't going down to children, it's going UP to a parent - what extra information would let you walk upward too?",
    "do one pass over the array building a parent index for every valid node, or equivalently build an adjacency list connecting each node to its parent and its valid children",
    "once you have that graph, this is just a standard multi-source-style BFS starting from a single source: the target node",
    "run BFS for exactly k layers (or track distance-so-far and stop expanding once a popped node's distance equals k), collecting every value at distance exactly k, then sort the collected values"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "only exploring children and forgetting to also connect back to the parent, which misses nodes that are 'behind' the target",
    "revisiting the node you just came from (going child -> parent -> same child again), causing an infinite loop or wrong distances without a visited set",
    "returning nodes at distance <= k instead of exactly k"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A binary tree is given as a plain integer array named `tree`, using a complete-binary-tree (heap) index layout:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node here\" - real node values always fall between -100 and 100, so -101 can never be a genuine value\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2 has no left child (sentinel) but does have a right child, 4.\n\nYou're also given a node value `target` (guaranteed to exist somewhere in the tree) and an integer `k`.\n\n**Treating the tree as an undirected graph (you can walk from a node to its parent as well as to its children), find every node whose distance from the target node is exactly `k`, and return their values sorted in ascending order.**\n\nDistance is measured as the number of edges on the shortest path.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= tree.length <= 2000",
      "-100 <= tree[i] <= 100, or tree[i] == -101 marking a missing node",
      "0 <= k <= 1000",
      "target is guaranteed to equal a real value present in tree"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "k = 0, which should return just the target's own value",
      "k larger than any actual distance in the tree, which should return an empty list",
      "target is the root, so the search only has children to expand into, no parent"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
